  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the numbers $\\sqrt{20},$ $\\sqrt{5},$ and $\\sqrt{11}.$ Write these three numbers in order from least to greatest.\n\nThe numbers, in order from least to greatest, are:\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find two consecutive whole numbers that bracket each root. We do this by finding the perfect squares just below and just above the number under the radical.\n\n- For $\\sqrt{5}:$ \nSince $4<5<9,$ we have $\\sqrt{4}<\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{9}.$ This means $2<\\sqrt{5}<3.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{11}:$\nSince $9<11<16,$ we have $\\sqrt{9}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{16}.$ This means $3<\\sqrt{11}<4.$\n\n- For $\\sqrt{20}:$\nSince $16<20<25,$ we have $\\sqrt{16}<\\sqrt{20}<\\sqrt{25}.$ This means $4<\\sqrt{20}<5.$\n\nFrom these bounds, we can see that all three numbers fall into different unit intervals:\n\n$$\n2<\\sqrt{5}<3<\\sqrt{11}<4<\\sqrt{20}<5\n$$\n\nComparing these intervals, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}<\\sqrt{11}<\\sqrt{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the numbers in order from least to greatest are $\\sqrt{5},$ $\\sqrt{11},$ and $\\sqrt{20}.$",
